Third-party components
----------------------
Elozi depends on open-source packages that keep their own licenses. Most are
permissive (MIT, ISC, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, Apache-2.0, 0BSD,
BlueOak-1.0.0, Unlicense). Two cases deserve attention:

1. WhatsApp connector -> GPL-3.0 code.
   The WhatsApp QR connector uses @whiskeysockets/baileys (MIT), which depends
   on libsignal (GPL-3.0) for the Signal protocol encryption. The MIT license
   of Elozi does not change libsignal's license. In plain words: if you
   distribute a build that bundles Baileys/libsignal (for example a Docker
   image, installer or tarball with node_modules), the libsignal part comes
   with GPL-3.0 obligations (offer its source and keep its license notice).
   Distributing only the Elozi source code (without node_modules) does not
   bundle libsignal, but libsignal itself is always GPL-3.0. If you need a
   distribution without GPL-3.0 code, remove the WhatsApp QR connector and
   use the official WhatsApp Business Cloud API instead.

2. Image processing -> LGPL-3.0 binaries.
   sharp (Apache-2.0), used by Baileys, loads prebuilt libvips binaries
   (@img/sharp-libvips-*, LGPL-3.0-or-later) as unmodified, replaceable shared
   libraries. Keep their license notices when you redistribute them.

Other notable components
